    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Chimú built Chan Chan around 850 CE as the capital of Chimor, a conquest state on the Peruvian coast, and it held an estimated 40,000 to 60,000 people.</li><li>The city rested on canals that carried water from the Moche river, and its rulers lived in ten walled ciudadelas while artisans worked in small rooms that doubled as workshops.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> conquered the Chimú around 1470 and dispersed them, the Spaniards looted the city after Pizarro founded Trujillo in 1535, and excavations began in 1969.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Chimú held that the sun created three eggs: gold for the ruler and the elite, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> for the rulers' spouses, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> for everyone else.</p></aside>
    </section>

