      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 850</span><span class="rf-text">The Chimú build Chan Chan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">900</span><span class="rf-text">Chan Chan is the capital of the Chimor kingdom from about this year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1100</span><span class="rf-text">A catastrophic flood destroys many of the canals to the north</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1470</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> defeat the Chimú and incorporate Chimor into their empire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1535</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> founds the Spanish city of Trujillo nearby</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1969</span><span class="rf-text">Michael Moseley and Carol J. Mackey begin excavations</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1986</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designates Chan Chan a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> and lists it as in danger</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chan Chan lies at the mouth of the Moche Valley, in a particularly arid part of the coastal desert of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. There is almost no rain, so the main source of fresh water was rivers carrying runoff from the Andes, and that runoff allowed the control of land and water through irrigation systems. Unlike most coastal ruins in Peru, the city stands very close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>.</p><p>Chan Chan is believed to have been constructed around 850 CE by the Chimú. Chimor, a conquest state, developed from the Chimú culture that established itself along the Peruvian coast around 900 CE, and Chan Chan was its capital from 900 to 1470. At first the city relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> about 15 metres deep. A network of canals diverting water from the Moche river was then dug to increase the farmland around it, and once the canals were in place the city had the potential to grow substantially.</p><p>The meaning and the language of origin of the name remain unresolved. The place appears as Cauchan in the foundation act of the Trujillo town council of 1536, and the form Chanchan appears in documents in a stable way only from the mid-17th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city of Chan Chan spanned 20 square kilometres and had a dense urban centre of six square kilometres. It was the capital of Chimor with an estimated population of 40,000 to 60,000 people. The archaeological site is considered the largest adobe city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><p>The canals that made this growth possible could run as long as 20 miles down the Moche valley into the city. Many of the canals to the north were destroyed by a catastrophic flood around 1100 CE, which was the key motivation for the Chimú to refocus their economy on foreign resources rather than on subsistence farming.</p><p>Although Chan Chan was an agricultural city, its people were famous for their pottery and textiles, and many craftsmen designed fabrics, pots and ceramics there. The size of the monumental architecture indicates that a large labour force was available. The Huaca Arco Iris, described as the largest adobe monumental complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, is placed chronologically alongside the first structures built at Chan Chan, towards the end of the Middle Horizon in the Central Andes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro.jpg" alt="Francisco Pizarro" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Francisco_Pizarro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amable-Paul Coutan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chan Chan was the capital of the Chimor kingdom, a conquest state, from 900 to 1470. The coastal society was governed by a powerful ruler called the Chimucapac and was held together by strict control of water and by external threats. The city consisted of ten citadels, which suggests that Chan Chan had ten rulers, and each citadel was built for a ruler or a deity.</p><p>Around 1470 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> defeated the Chimú and incorporated Chimor into the Inca Empire. The Incas applied what is called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitma">mitma</a> system of ethnic dispersion, which moved Chimú civilians to places the Incas had recently conquered. One account holds that the Incas cut off the canals that fed the city, leaving its crops dying from lack of water, and that the irrigation system was one of the main reasons the Chimú were conquered.</p><p>Chan Chan fell into decline after the conquest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> founded the Spanish city of Trujillo in 1535, a little over sixty years later, and that city pushed Chan Chan further into the shadows.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chan Chan had a hierarchical society in which deities and rulers ranked highest and servants lowest. It held nobles, farmers, fishers, traders, servants and many craftsmen. The elite lived in the citadels. The rest of the people lived in small, irregular agglutinated rooms, known as SIARs, which also served as workshops.</p><p>The Chimú held that the sun created three eggs, gold for the ruler and the elite, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> for the rulers' spouses, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> for everyone else. They worshipped the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>, from which most of their food came, and they relied heavily on their irrigation system for crops. The Tschudi compound is believed to have been built in honour of a sea deity whose name in Quingnam is unknown, and a pond in its middle was probably used in religious ceremonies.</p><p>The people of Chan Chan supposedly spoke Quingnam, a language that became extinct after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> conquest and is poorly documented. No evidence exists for a written system. Anthropologists theorise that they kept records on knotted cords called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quipu">khipus</a>, as the Incas did, although no artefacts survive to confirm it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first blow to the city's water supply was the catastrophic flood of about 1100 CE, which destroyed many canals to the north. The Chimú responded by turning towards foreign resources rather than subsistence farming.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> conquest around 1470 ended Chan Chan's time as a capital. The Incas dispersed Chimú civilians to other conquered places, and according to one account they cut off the irrigation canals that ran down the Moche valley. After Pizarro founded Trujillo in 1535 the Spaniards looted the city, which was still well known for its riches. A sixteenth-century list of items taken from a burial tomb in Chan Chan records a treasure equivalent to 80,000 pesos of gold.</p><p>The adobe buildings continued to suffer afterwards. Many of the city's buildings have been destroyed. Heavy rains, flooding and strong winds erode the structures, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-nino">El Niño</a> storms damage the bases of the walls, where humidity leads to salt contamination and plant growth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chan Chan is now an archaeological site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of La Libertad, five kilometres west of Trujillo. Its ten walled ciudadelas housed ceremonial rooms, burial chambers, temples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> and residences for the Chimú rulers. The walls were adobe brick with a smooth surface carved with birds, fish, small mammals, crabs, turtles and nets, in a realistic and in a more stylised manner. The Tschudi compound had a single entrance and walls up to twelve metres high, wider at the base than at the top, in anticipation of earthquakes.</p><p>Michael Moseley and Carol J. Mackey began excavations in 1969, and the work continues under the Peruvian Instituto Nacional de Cultura. A map made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a> in 1974 was the newest until 2017. On 28 November 1986 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated Chan Chan a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> and placed it on the List of World Heritage in Danger. A conservation master plan was drawn up in 1998. The Italian Mission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> has worked at the site since 2002 and has made three-dimensional models of the Tschudi Palace and the Huaca Arco Iris.</p><p>Archaeologists have recently given Mochica names to the compounds, although the Chimor spoke Quingnam, which is unrelated to Mochica. This has been criticised as a denial of local history.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Chan Chan, on the desert coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> near Trujillo, was the capital of the Chimor kingdom from about 900 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> conquest around 1470. Its adobe citadels were looted by the Spaniards and are now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</em></p>

